WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.780
This video is sponsored
by CuriosityStream. Use the link in the description

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:09.680
to start watching thousands of documentaries
and get access to our new educational streaming

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:16.900
service, Nebula, at no extra cost. One week
every year - either in January or February

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:23.970
- Chinese train stations, bus terminals, roads,
and airports overflow with travelers. Chinese

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:29.789
New Year is the largest human migration on
earth, with nearly 400 million people returning

00:00:29.789 --> 00:00:36.809
home to their families. For comparison, Thanksgiving
sees 50 million travelers, the Arba'een Pilgrimage,

00:00:36.809 --> 00:00:43.420
40 million, and India’s Kumbh Mela, 30.
Rather than give workers a yearly allowance

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:49.059
of paid leave, Chinese factories and companies
shut down completely for two weeks of the

00:00:49.059 --> 00:00:54.709
year: around the Lunar New Year and National
Day. These ‘Golden Weeks’ test the limits

00:00:54.709 --> 00:00:59.869
of China’s world-class transportation network.
Many of the country’s notoriously-high toll

00:00:59.869 --> 00:01:06.180
fees which are normally designed to disincentivize
driving, are suspended, creating 40-kilometer

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:13.320
or 25-mile traffic jams. Train and plane tickets
sell out in minutes, prices skyrocket, and,

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:20.150
then, the celebrations begin. Kids receive
lucky red envelopes filled with cash, incense

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:26.990
is burned for ancestors, and fireworks light
up the sky. In 2019, the year of the pig was

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:33.820
celebrated with giant statues in malls, parade
floats, billboards, and, in Hong Kong, pig

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:41.380
nose-shaped fireworks. The only things missing
were actual… pigs. In the last two years,

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:47.810
a third China’s pigs have died in the largest
ever animal disease outbreak, causing pork

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:53.300
prices to more than double. Now, losing such
a large source of food would be disastrous

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:59.390
anywhere, but for the largest pork-consuming
country on the planet, under an administration

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:04.170
whose power hinges on its ability to raise
the standard of living, during a trade war

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:09.080
with the country who supplies its pig feed,
is a crisis some consider more dangerous for

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:15.400
Beijing than the Taiwanese presidential elections,
the ban on Huawei, or, even the Hong Kong protests.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:23.800
Pigs are the ideal farm animals.
They don’t require much space, they bear

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:30.200
about 12 piglets twice a year, and they eat
just about anything. Because of this, they’ve

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:36.050
long been an important part of the Chinese
diet. But not for the reason you might expect.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:41.959
Mao Zedong called pigs “fertilizer factories
on four legs”, ordering they be fed leftover

00:02:41.959 --> 00:02:47.010
household scraps to produce high-nutrient
manure which would, in turn, produce higher

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:53.489
grain yields. So, while nearly every family
owned&nbsp;pigs, eating pork was a rare luxury,

00:02:53.489 --> 00:02:59.439
reserved for holidays like the New Year. Today,
pork is still seen as a luxury, but one that

00:02:59.439 --> 00:03:05.659
everyone now expects. About half of the world’s
pork is consumed in China, and that’s not

00:03:05.659 --> 00:03:12.459
just because of its large population. Adjusted
per capita, China, Hong Kong, and Macau eat

00:03:12.459 --> 00:03:19.689
the 4th most pork in the world. Even more
impressive, that’s despite China’s $16,807

00:03:19.689 --> 00:03:28.730
Per Capita GDP, compared to 3rd place, the
European Union’s $41,091. Pork accounts

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:36.370
for two-thirds of all meat consumed in China,
with poultry accounting for 21%, beef, 8%,

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:41.650
and lamb, 5. But even these numbers don’t
adequately explain the Chinese obsession with

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:48.809
pigs. Throughout the country, pork is an almost
default ingredient. Dim Sum, dumplings, rice,

00:03:48.809 --> 00:03:54.150
noodles, soup, even, sometimes, desert, contains
pork, whether it’s listed on the menu or

00:03:54.150 --> 00:04:01.060
not. The Chinese word for meat, “ròu”
literally means pork. All other types of meat

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:08.930
have to be specified. Cows, for example, are
“niú”, so beef is “niú ròu”. Fish,

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:15.389
“Yú”, becomes “Yú ròu”. And so
on. The Chinese character for family or home,

00:04:15.389 --> 00:04:21.200
“Jiā” is composed of two elements, or
radicals - the character for roof underneath

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:27.370
the character for pig. All of this is to say:
pork is more than just a meal in China - it’s

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:33.810
ingrained in the culture. Which is why its
outbreak of African Swine Fever is so troubling.

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:39.080
The good news is that the virus isn’t dangerous
to humans. The bad news is that it’s basically

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:45.400
pig Ebola. The difference is that Ebola isn’t
airborne, only remains active for a few days

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:52.780
at room temperature, and only has about a
50% fatality rate. African Swine Fever, meanwhile,

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:58.170
spreads like wildfire. It’s found in all
the body fluids and tissues of its victims,

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:03.470
who experience vomiting, high temperatures,
and difficulty breathing. Some variants have

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:09.280
a 100% mortality rate. But death doesn’t
stop the virus from spreading. It lives in

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:15.610
feces and urine for days and can be spread
through flies, ticks, insects, vehicles, equipment

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:21.500
or clothing. And because there’s no vaccination,
the only solution is to kill and bury all

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:29.020
infected animals. As of 2019, the virus has
reached Vietnam, Cambodia, Mongolia, North

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:35.330
Korea, Hong Kong, and every province of China.
Official Chinese government sources estimate

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:41.780
$140 billion in direct losses and the death
of a quarter of the world’s pigs. Although

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:47.430
these numbers are likely inaccurate. Because
government subsidies for affected farms are

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:53.620
set below-market prices, both local government
officials and farmers themselves are incentivized

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:58.840
to hide and under-report the scale of the
outbreak. Regardless, the central government

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:04.730
simply doesn’t have many options. Many countries
keep emergency supplies of their national

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:11.260
obsessions: Canada has the Strategic Maple
Syrup Reserve, The U.S. has the Petroleum

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:18.180
Reserve, and China, since 2008, has the Strategic
Pork Reserve, comprised of live farms and

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:23.570
dozens of highly-guarded storehouses where
packaged pork is frozen and rotated. When

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:29.080
prices rise, supplies fall, or in the days
before the New Year, the government releases

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:34.940
some of its supply onto the market. The exact
amount of pork kept is a national secret but

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:40.330
is said to be around 200,000 tons, which,
compared to the country’s demand for 50

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:45.930
million tons every year, hardly makes a difference
during a crisis of this magnitude. The government

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:51.080
has lifted import bans, tried breeding giant
pigs, and some restaurants have resorted to

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:57.830
cooking with dog or rabbit meat. High pork
prices present Beijing with not just an economic

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:04.280
but also a political problem. For older generations,
being able to eat a once annual treat every

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:10.770
day is a symbol and reminder of their country’s
progress. When pork is unavailable or too

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:16.640
costly, on the other hand, it awakens grim
memories of China’s dark and hungry past.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:21.410
While international attention focuses on stories
like Hong Kong’s protests, it’s easy to

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:27.520
forget that fighting for democracy is a privilege
afforded to the already moderately well-off.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:32.460
For millions of Chinese, the number one concern
is putting food on the table, and losing their

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:38.150
favorite source of protein is more than merely
inconvenient. Even with reports of slowing

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:44.030
inflation, it will undoubtedly take years
for China’s pig population to fully recover.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:48.710
In the future, China is expected to shift
more of its pig farming to large, industrial

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:54.670
factories, giving it more direct control over
their operations. There’s one silver lining,

00:07:54.670 --> 00:08:00.730
however: the outbreak provides a golden opportunity
for China to embrace artificial and more sustainably

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:06.810
grown meat. Less than 2% of China’s population
is currently vegetarian, and eating meat is

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:12.760
very much part of Chinese life. So too, however,
is tea, and yet, companies like Starbucks

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:19.640
and Luckin’ Coffee have seen explosive growth
in China. In one survey of 30 countries, 73%

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:24.460
of Chinese respondents said they would eat
a plant-based meat substitute - higher than

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:29.910
any other country, including India. And China’s
Ministry of Health released guidelines in

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:37.320
2016 recommending people cut their meat consumption
in half by 2020. No one expects China to suddenly

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:42.940
become the leader in artificially grown meat,
but, twenty years ago, neither did anyone

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:48.760
expect it to become the electric vehicle capital
of the world, or lay more high-speed rail

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:55.470
than the rest of the world combined, or use
almost exclusively mobile payments, and yet

00:08:55.470 --> 00:09:01.890
here we are. China’s authoritarian model
has led to miraculously rapid technological

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:08.110
progress. The pig could be next. There’s
another story about China I’m excited to

